# Addition where the two operands already share cells.
#
#   rw progs/padd.rw '<zyww>'   ->   10100     (1100 + 1000, in one string)
#
# Every other arithmetic program here keeps its operands apart and sends a
# traveller between them, which is why 97% of a multiply in binmul.rw is one
# symbol stepping past another. This one asks what happens if the operands are
# interleaved instead, so a column is a single cell and nothing has to travel.
#
# A cell holds one bit of each number, read as (a,r):
#
#   w = (0,0)   x = (0,1)   y = (1,0)   z = (1,1)
#
# The answer replaces r. The carry is the only thing that moves, and it moves
# the shortest distance a carry can: one cell, to the column above.
#
#   binadd.rw    8 bits     84 rewrites      256 bits   66,052
#   padd.rw      8 bits     20 rewrites      256 bits      516
#
# Doubling the width costs binadd.rw 3.97x and this 1.98x, so the gap is not a
# constant -- separating the operands costs a factor of the width itself.
#
# It does not replace binadd.rw. Interleaving two numbers that arrive apart
# costs a quadratic shuffle of its own, so this only pays when the operands
# are already in this shape and stay in it, as in a systolic layout.

>->M

# one column: cell, carry in -> cell with r replaced, carry out.
# M carries nothing, N carries one.
wM->Mw
xM->Mx
yM->Mz
zM->Ny
wN->Mx
xN->Nw
yN->Ny
zN->Nz

# out of the top: a carry becomes a new leading digit
<M->
<N-><x

# read off the answer, which is the r of every cell
w->0
x->1
y->0
z->1
<->.
